---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE, LEGAL, SISTER_SITES, CLAIMS } from '../lib/site.js';
import { getSiteStats, formatCount } from '../lib/data.js';
import { guides } from '../lib/guides.js';
import { glossary } from '../lib/glossary.js';
import { organizationSchema } from '../lib/schema.js';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/schema.js';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'About', url: '/about/' },
];
const stats = getSiteStats();
const schemas = [breadcrumbSchema(crumbs), organizationSchema()];
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`About ${SITE.name}: Who Writes It, How It Is Funded`}
  description={`Who runs ${SITE.name}, how the garage door directory is built, how cost figures are researched, and what we will and will not claim about a listing.`}
  canonical="/about/"
  schemas={schemas}
>
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

    <article style="margin-top:1rem;" class="prose">
      <h1 style="margin:1rem 0 1rem;">About {SITE.name}</h1>

      <p>
        {SITE.name} does two things. It publishes what garage door work actually costs in
        2026, and it lists the garage door companies operating in a given city. Both are
        free, and no business pays to be included or promoted.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p>
        Garage door repair is a trade where the customer is usually at their worst
        negotiating position. The door has failed, the car is stuck, it is often a weekend,
        and almost nobody knows whether $600 for a pair of springs is a fair price or
        double. The information asymmetry is the whole problem.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the first thing this site publishes is the price. {guides.length} guides cover
        what each job costs, what drives the number, and where the arithmetic tips from
        repair to replacement — plus a {glossary.length}-term
        {' '}<a href="/glossary/">glossary</a> so you can read the quote you are handed.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will and will not claim</h2>
      <p>
        {CLAIMS.listingMeaning} We do not rank businesses by quality, and we do not publish
        star ratings — not as an oversight, but because no data source we are legally
        permitted to republish contains any. That is explained in full in our
        {' '}<a href="/editorial-guidelines/">editorial guidelines</a>, along with the exact
        quality bar a listing has to clear.
      </p>
      <p>
        In California, Florida and Arizona — the only three states that license garage door
        work as its own trade — some listings show a verified licence badge.
        {' '}{CLAIMS.licenceMeaning} In the other 47 states there is no garage door register
        to check, and those pages say so plainly rather than leaving you to assume a check
        happened.
      </p>

      {stats.businesses > 0 && (
        <>
          <h2>Where it is up to</h2>
          <p>
            {formatCount(stats.businesses)} listings across {formatCount(stats.cities)}
            {' '}cities in {formatCount(stats.states)} states, every one with a working phone
            number. The directory is being built metro by metro rather than dumped
            nationally in one go, because a city page with two listings on it is not worth
            publishing.
          </p>
        </>
      )}

      <h2>Who writes it</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is written and run by {LEGAL.founderName}, {LEGAL.founderRole}. The
        cost research is done in-house against contractor pricing, manufacturer
        documentation and trade sources, and every guide carries a visible last-updated
        date.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are not garage door technicians and we do not pretend to be. What we do is
        research prices carefully, read the licensing rules properly, and say exactly how
        confident we are in each. Where the right answer is "call a professional", the page
        says that instead of walking you into a job that can break your arm.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is funded</h2>
      <p>
        Advertising, and in future affiliate arrangements. Neither affects which businesses
        appear or in what order — there is no mechanism for a business to pay us for
        placement, because we have not built one. If we ever have a commercial relationship
        that could reasonably change how you read a page, it will be disclosed on that page.
      </p>

      {SISTER_SITES.disclose && (
        <>
          <h2>Related sites</h2>
          <p>
            The same operator publishes
            {' '}{SISTER_SITES.sites.map((s, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 ? (i === SISTER_SITES.sites.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ') : ''}
                <a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.name}</a> ({s.trade})
              </>
            ))}. They are separate sites with separate editorial, disclosed here so common
            ownership is not something you have to discover.
          </p>
        </>
      )}

      <h2>Corrections and contact</h2>
      <p>
        If a figure is wrong, a listing is out of date, or you own a listed business and
        want the entry corrected or removed, email
        {' '}<a href={`mailto:${LEGAL.contactEmail}`}>{LEGAL.contactEmail}</a> or use the
        {' '}<a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. We correct errors on the page rather than
        deleting them quietly.
      </p>
      <p class="claim-note">
        {LEGAL.legalName}, {LEGAL.address}.
        {' '}<a href="/terms/">Terms of service</a> ·
        {' '}<a href="/privacy/">Privacy policy</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
